mruby gap: `defined?` -- the keyword form (`defined? expr` / `defined?(expr)`)
-- is not implemented at all. Vendored mruby's lexer never recognized
`defined?` as a keyword, so `defined?(Foo)` parsed as an ordinary method
call named `defined?` and raised NoMethodError at runtime instead of
answering the question at compile time. Real, not vendor-specific: upstream
mruby 3.3.0 has never implemented this keyword either (confirmed directly
against its own mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/{codegen.c,parse.y,keywords}).
This project's own maintainer had evidently started addressing it before
this patch -- the NODE_DEFINED enum value and mrb_ast_defined_node AST node
already existed in node.h, along with a codegen.c stub that unconditionally
returned nil and commented "defined? is complex to implement correctly" --
but never finished wiring up the lexer, grammar, or a real codegen.

Found via a real VX Ace game's speech-bubble ("吹きだしウィンドウ") add-on
(docs/rpgvx-rgss-api-gap.md), which guards a SceneManager lookup with
`defined?(SceneManager)` and crashed with NoMethodError on the very first
frame it ran.

This patch wires up all three missing pieces:

  1. Lexer (mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/keywords, a gperf source): adds
     `defined?` as a keyword. The literal `?` is included in the gperf key
     because mruby's lexer already accumulates a trailing `?`/`!` into the
     token text before calling `mrb_reserved_word` (parse.y), so this
     overrides the token that would otherwise be assigned to it (a
     function-identifier-ending-in-`?`, i.e. an ordinary method-call parse).

  2. Grammar (mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/parse.y): adds `keyword_defined`
     alongside `keyword_not`, whose two existing forms (`not expr` at the
     `expr` level, `not(expr)`/`not()` at the `primary` level) serve as the
     direct structural template. Only the non-parenthesized `expr`-level
     form and the parenthesized `primary`-level form are added (matching
     real Ruby's own two `defined?` forms, per CRuby's parse.y); a
     `new_defined` constructor wraps the inner expression in the
     pre-existing NODE_DEFINED/mrb_ast_defined_node AST node. Precedence is
     `%nonassoc`, placed right after `%right keyword_not`, matching real
     CRuby's own `%nonassoc keyword_defined` placement.

  3. Codegen (mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/codegen.c): replaces the
     always-nil `codegen_defined` stub with real per-node-type logic,
     dispatching on the wrapped expression's node type. The literal/
     compile-time-certain cases (nil/true/false/self, an already-declared
     local variable, any assignment form) are pure string/nil pushes with
     zero evaluation -- matching real Ruby's own behavior of never actually
     performing the assignment in `defined?(x = 1)`, while `x` still becomes
     a known local afterward because mruby's parser (like CRuby's) registers
     locals at parse time, independent of whether that code path ever runs.
     Constant reads (NODE_CONST/COLON2/COLON3) reuse the normal read codegen
     wrapped in a compiler-generated rescue region (the same
     catch_handler_new/catch_handler_set/OP_EXCEPT mechanism codegen_rescue
     uses) since constant reads have no side effects, so catching and
     discarding any exception is safe. Method calls (NODE_CALL, which also
     covers operators -- `1 + 1` is a NODE_CALL under the hood) emit
     `<receiver-or-self>.respond_to?(:method_name, true)` rather than
     invoking the method, matching real Ruby's `defined?(raise "x")` =>
     "method" (never raises). Instance/class/global variables and `yield`
     each emit a single boolean-returning self-send
     (instance_variable_defined?/class_variable_defined?/
     global_variables.include?/block_given?) and convert the boolean to the
     literal answer string or nil. Every helper here that emits an
     OP_SEND/OP_SSEND does one extra push() beyond the receiver and its
     arguments before resetting cursp() to the call's base register: those
     opcodes read a block-argument slot at `a + n + 1` even when no block is
     passed (mrb_vm_exec's `bidx` in vm.c), so nregs must account for that
     trailing register or the VM's own `bidx < irep->nregs` assertion fails
     at runtime despite the bytecode itself being well-formed -- this is
     exactly the register-allocation risk class of bug this project has hit
     before with hand-written codegen, so it is called out explicitly here
     and in a comment at its first occurrence in the patch itself. The
     default fallback (any other expression: literals, `if`/`case` used as
     an expression, etc.) pushes "expression", matching real Ruby's own
     default answer for anything it does not special-case.

     Two intentional, documented simplifications versus real Ruby, both
     safe (neither ever evaluates anything with side effects):
       - Ruby recursively checks the definedness of array-literal elements
         and call arguments (`defined?([undefined_var])` => nil); this
         implementation does not recurse and answers from the outer node
         type alone ("expression"/"method" respectively).
       - `super`/`zsuper` conservatively answer nil rather than attempting
         to determine the current method context at compile time.

  4. mrblib/kernel.rb: adds one small private Kernel helper,
     `__defined_cvar_scope`, used only by the class-variable case above.
     Real Ruby resolves `@@cvar` via the lexical scope (cref) chain; a
     `self.class`-based approximation is wrong specifically for a
     class/singleton method (`def self.foo; defined?(@@cv); end` has `self`
     already equal to the class itself, and `self.class` there is `Class`,
     which knows nothing about the subject class's own `@@cv`). This helper
     picks `self` when `self` is already a Module/Class and `self.class`
     otherwise, covering both the plain-instance-method and
     class/singleton-method cases (still not the full lexical-cref chain
     for every possible nesting -- see its own comment).

Verified against the full `rake test` suite (1847 OK / 0 KO / 0 Crash, no
regressions) and a dedicated regression test
(mruby-rgss/test/test.rb, "defined? matches real Ruby's answers for each
expression shape") whose expected values were checked directly against a
real CRuby 3.3.6 interpreter for every case, not from documentation alone.
